---
import Footer from '../components/landing/Footer.astro';
import Nav from '../components/landing/Nav.astro';
import Landing from '../layouts/Landing.astro';
import '../styles/page.css';

const repo = 'https://github.com/wsoule/dispatch';
---

<Landing
  title="Docs: Dispatch"
  description="Install Dispatch, put a repo on the board, connect Claude Code or any MCP agent, and dispatch your first task."
  path="/docs"
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <h1>Quickstart</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          From install to an agent landing its first task, in a repo you already have. The{' '}
          <a href={`${repo}#readme`}>README</a> goes deeper on every step.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>1. Install</h2>
        <p>On macOS, with Homebrew:</p>
        <pre><code>brew install --cask wsoule/tap/dispatch</code></pre>
        <p>
          Or download an installer from the <a href={`${repo}/releases/latest`}>latest release</a>:
          macOS (Apple Silicon and Intel, signed and notarized) and Linux (<code>.deb</code>,{' '}
          <code>.rpm</code>, <code>.AppImage</code>). Installing the app also puts the{' '}
          <code>dispatch</code> command on your path.
        </p>

        <h2>2. Put a repo on the board</h2>
        <p>In any git repository:</p>
        <pre><code>dispatch init
dispatch task create "My first task" --priority high
dispatch task list</code></pre>
        <p>
          Then open the Dispatch app and point it at the repo. <code>dispatch init</code> creates{' '}
          <code>.dispatch/</code> with the project's settings. Tasks are markdown files in{' '}
          <code>.dispatch/tasks/</code> by default, or, with <code>dispatch init --db</code>, rows in
          a local database with a git receipt log kept outside your repo.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Connect your agents</h2>
        <p>
          <code>dispatch init</code> registers Dispatch's MCP server in the project's{' '}
          <code>.mcp.json</code>. Start Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI or any MCP agent in the repo
          and it can read the board, claim the next task, comment, and save what it learned:
        </p>
        <pre><code>claude "Take the next task on the Dispatch board and post your plan on it."</code></pre>
        <p>
          A new agent waits for your approval before it writes shared memory. You'll see it in{' '}
          <strong>Coming in</strong>.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Dispatch a task</h2>
        <p>
          In the app, open a task and press <strong>Dispatch</strong>, or from the terminal:
        </p>
        <pre><code>dispatch run t-1a2b3c</code></pre>
        <p>
          Each run works in its own git worktree, on its own branch, inside the paths the task
          declared it would write. Anything else needs your approval while it runs. Runs carry
          spend and turn caps. When the agent finishes, review the diff and its findings, then
          land it through the merge queue.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Plan bigger work</h2>
        <p>
          Tell the <strong>Overseer</strong> what you want built, in one sentence. It proposes a
          milestone with tasks and dependencies. Nothing is created until you approve. From the
          milestone's plan, <strong>Send agents</strong> works it wave by wave: a blocked task
          starts on its own once its blockers finish. The <strong>Graph</strong> shows what stands
          between you and a release, and copies as Mermaid.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Bring your team</h2>
        <pre><code>dispatch team start
dispatch team invite teammate@example.com</code></pre>
        <p>
          Your teammate runs <code>dispatch team join</code> with the link it prints. Their agents
          share your board and can message yours. Free for up to three people, with every
          feature; see <a href="/pricing">pricing</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Where things live</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Your code</strong> stays in your checkout. Runs use your machine and your own agent and API key.</li>
          <li><strong>Tasks and history</strong> live in your repo or in a local database whose receipt log is a git repository you can restore from.</li>
          <li><strong>Nothing is uploaded.</strong> The daemon serves the app over local HTTP. Teams sync through a git branch, or a relay that can't read what it carries.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>More</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={`${repo}#readme`}>README</a>: storage, migration, terminals, files, Design mode, remotes.</li>
          <li><a href="/protocol/">Dispatch Messaging Protocol</a>: how agents talk to each other.</li>
          <li><a href="/changelog">Changelog</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Landing>
